          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is the difference between flu and a cold?",
          "answer": "**Short answer:** Flu usually starts suddenly and hits harder. A cold builds up gradually and mostly affects the nose and throat. The two can be hard to tell apart in any one person.\n\n**How they differ** (from the *Influenza* and *Upper respiratory infection (common cold)* summaries):\n\n- **Onset:** Flu often begins abruptly, and people can often name the hour it started. A cold tends to creep in over a few days.\n- **Symptoms:** Flu typically brings fever, chills, muscle aches, headache, marked tiredness and a dry cough. A cold is mainly nasal symptoms and sore throat, and rarely causes high fever or severe exhaustion in adults.\n- **Severity:** Flu is more severe in both symptoms and consequences. It causes substantial hospitalization and deaths each season, especially in older adults, young children, pregnant people and people with long-term conditions. Most colds resolve on their own in otherwise healthy people.\n\n**When to seek care:** The passages I retrieved do not list specific seek-care criteria for flu or colds, so I can't give you a full list from this source. Some general points that do appear in the material:\n\n- If you have flu and are in a higher-risk group, or your illness is getting worse, talk to a clinician promptly. Guidance on antiviral treatment says it works best when started within 48 hours of symptoms starting, and that it is recommended as early as possible for people who are hospitalized, severely ill, or at higher risk of complications. Whether it suits you is a decision to make with a clinician.\n- The sepsis guidance lists \"a feeling of being more unwell than ever before\" as a warning sign of a serious response to infection. If you feel that way, get urgent medical help rather than waiting.\n\nFor a more complete guide to when to seek care, ask a pharmacist, your doctor, or a local health service. If you have trouble breathing, chest pain, confusion, or are very drowsy or hard to wake, call emergency services now.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
